</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue? · 为什么天空是蓝色的？</h1>
  <div class="sub">One answer, three audiences — in English and Chinese · 一个答案，三种受众</div>
</header>

<div class="toolbar" role="group" aria-label="Language view">
  <button id="btn-both" class="active" onclick="setView('both')">EN + 中文</button>
  <button id="btn-en" onclick="setView('en')">English only</button>
  <button id="btn-zh" onclick="setView('zh')">仅中文</button>
</div>

<main id="content" class="both">

  <!-- ============ ENGLISH COLUMN ============ -->
  <section class="col col-en" lang="en">
    <div class="lang-title">English</div>

    <article class="card toddler">
      <h2><span class="badge">AGE 3</span> For a 3-Year-Old</h2>
      <p>Sunlight is actually a mix of lots of colors hiding together — like a secret rainbow! When the sunlight zooms through the air, the little tiny blue bits bounce around everywhere, just like little bouncy balls. All that bouncing blue light bounces right into your eyes, so the sky looks blue! 🌈</p>
      <p>At sunset, the sunlight has to travel through a whole <em>ton</em> more air to reach you, and the blue bounces away before it gets there — so you see warm oranges and pinks instead.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card teen">
      <h2><span class="badge">HIGH SCHOOL</span> For a High School Teenager</h2>
      <p>Sunlight is white light: a combination of all visible wavelengths. When it enters Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with molecules of nitrogen and oxygen — which are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light — causing <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>.</p>
      <p>Rayleigh scattering intensity scales as:</p>
      <div class="formula">I &prop; <span class="frac"><span class="top">1</span><span class="bot">&lambda;<sup>4</sup></span></span></div>
      <p>Shorter wavelengths scatter far more strongly than longer ones. Blue light (~450 nm) scatters roughly (650/450)&sup4; &asymp; <strong>5.5&times; more</strong> than red light (~650 nm), so the scattered light that reaches your eyes from every direction in the sky is dominated by blue.</p>
      <p>Why doesn't the sky look violet then? Violet has an even shorter wavelength and scatters even more. Two reasons: (1) the Sun emits less violet than blue (its spectrum peaks in the visible but drops off beyond blue), and (2) our eyes are less sensitive to violet — we have very few violet-sensitive cone cells, and our lenses filter some of it.</p>
      <p><strong>Bonus — sunsets:</strong> at low solar angles, light travels through ~10&times; more atmosphere. Blue is scattered out of the line of sight almost entirely, leaving the direct beam reddened, which is why sunsets are orange and red.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card phd">
      <h2><span class="badge">PHD</span> For a Physics PhD</h2>
      <p>The color of the clear sky arises from Rayleigh scattering of solar radiation by atmospheric molecules, where the relevant dimensionless size parameter x&nbsp;=&nbsp;2&pi;r/&lambda; is well below unity (r&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;0.15 nm for N<sub>2</sub>, &lambda; ~ 400–700 nm), so the dipole approximation is valid and the cross section is:</p>
      <div class="formula">
        &sigma;(&lambda;) =
        <span class="frac"><span class="top">8&pi;<sup>3</sup></span><span class="bot">3</span></span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="top">n<sup>2</sup> &minus; 1</span><span class="bot">N &lambda;<sup>2</sup></span></span>
        &middot; f(&lambda;) &middot;
        <span class="frac"><span class="top">6(2 + 3&delta;)</span><span class="bot">5(2 &minus; &delta;)</span></span>
      </div>
      <p>where N is the number density, f(&lambda;) the dispersion factor, and &delta; the depolarization factor (~0.03 for air). The classical &lambda;<sup>&minus;4</sup> scaling emerges in this limit; more precisely, dispersion corrections and the refractive-index dependence introduce mild deviations.</p>
      <p>A rigorous treatment starts from the time-dependent induced dipole p(t)&nbsp;=&nbsp;&alpha;&nbsp;E(t) of a bound, polarizable electron system. Averaging over molecular orientation gives the anisotropy of the scattered radiation, which is responsible for the ~3% polarization of the clear sky (polarizers show maximum polarization ~90&deg; from the Sun).</p>
      <p>Key caveats the &lambda;<sup>&minus;4</sup> law alone misses:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Non-ideality:</strong> the full Mie/Rayleigh transition, plus the refractive index of air n(&lambda;)&nbsp;&lt;&nbsp;1, corrects the naive cross section. The Rayleigh cross section for air at 550 nm is &asymp; 5.1 &times; 10<sup>&minus;31</sup> m<sup>2</sup> per molecule.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple scattering:</strong> single-scattering is a good first approximation at sea level for a clear sky, but for deep atmosphere (e.g., high altitude, twilight) multiple scattering and Raman scattering (airglow, the CH<sub>4</sub>/OH lines) contribute.</li>
        <li><strong>Spectral source and detection:</strong> the sky's color is a convolution of the solar spectrum (&asymp; 5777 K blackbody modified by telluric absorption), the scattering phase function, atmospheric transmittance, and the human L/M/S cone sensitivities — which is precisely why it appears blue rather than violet despite greater violet scattering.</li>
        <li><strong>Aerosols and humidity:</strong> Mie scattering by aerosols (r &sucr; &lambda;) is nearly wavelength-independent and adds a whitish component; this is why hazy skies look paler.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In short: Rayleigh scattering in the dipole limit, convolved with solar spectral radiance and photopic response, with Mie and multiple-scattering corrections that set the pale tone of real skies.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ CHINESE COLUMN ============ -->
  <section class="col col-zh" lang="zh-Hans">
    <div class="lang-title zh">中文（简体）</div>

    <article class="card toddler">
      <h2><span class="badge">3岁</span> 给三岁小朋友</h2>
      <p>阳光其实是很多种颜色藏在一起——就像一道藏在里面的彩虹！当阳光飞快地穿过空气时，那些小小的蓝色光斑到处乱弹，就像很多小球在蹦来蹦去。这些乱弹的蓝光正好弹进你的眼睛里，所以天空看起来就是蓝色的啦！🌈</p>
      <p>到了日落的时候，阳光要穿过更多的空气才能到你眼前，蓝色早弹走了，所以你会看到暖暖的橙色和粉色。</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card teen">
      <h2><span class="badge">高中</span> 给高中生</h2>
      <p>阳光是白光，由所有可见光波长混合而成。当它进入地球大气层后，会与氮和氧分子发生相互作用——这些分子比可见光的波长远小得多——从而产生<strong>瑞利散射（Rayleigh scattering）</strong>。</p>
      <p>瑞利散射的强度随波长变化为：</p>
      <div class="formula">I &prop; <span class="frac"><span class="top">1</span><span class="bot">&lambda;<sup>4</sup></span></span></div>
      <p>波长越短，散射越强得多。蓝光（约 450 纳米）的散射强度大约是红光（约 650 纳米）的 (650/450)&sup4; &asymp; <strong>5.5 倍</strong>，因此从天空各个方向进入你眼睛的散射光以蓝色为主。</p>
      <p>那为什么天空不是紫色的呢？紫光波长更短，散射得更厉害，但有两个原因：(1) 太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少（其可见光谱峰值在蓝光以外迅速衰减）；(2) 人眼对紫光不敏感——我们几乎没有感知紫光的锥体细胞，而且眼镜头部还会过滤掉一部分紫光。</p>
      <p><strong>附加内容——日落：</strong> 当太阳位置很低时，光要穿过大约 10 倍厚的大气层。蓝光几乎完全被散射出视线之外，剩下直达你的光束偏红，所以日落呈橙色和红色。</p>
    </article>

    <article class="card phd">
      <h2><span class="badge">博士</span> 给物理学博士</h2>
      <p>晴天的天空颜色源于太阳辐射在大气分子上的瑞利散射。此时无量纲尺寸参数 x&nbsp;=&nbsp;2&pi;r/&lambda; 远小于 1（N<sub>2</sub> 的 r&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;0.15 nm，&lambda; ~ 400–700 nm），偶极近似成立，散射截面为：</p>
      <div class="formula">
        &sigma;(&lambda;) =
        <span class="frac"><span class="top">8&pi;<sup>3</sup></span><span class="bot">3</span></span>
        <span class="frac"><span class="top">n<sup>2</sup> &minus; 1</span><span class="bot">N &lambda;<sup>2</sup></span></span>
        &middot; f(&lambda;) &middot;
        <span class="frac"><span class="top">6(2 + 3&delta;)</span><span class="bot">5(2 &minus; &delta;)</span></span>
      </div>
      <p>其中 N 为数密度，f(&lambda;) 为色散因子，&delta; 为去偏振因子（空气约为 0.03）。在该极限下自然导出经典的 &lambda;<sup>&minus;4</sup> 标度律；更精确地说，色散修正和折射率依赖关系会引入轻微的偏离。</p>
      <p>严格的推导从束缚、可极化电子体系在时变电场下的诱导偶极矩 p(t)&nbsp;=&nbsp;&alpha;&nbsp;E(t) 出发。对分子取向求平均后得到散射辐射的各向异性，这正是晴天天空约 3% 偏振度的来源（偏振片在距太阳约 90&deg; 方向显示最大偏振）。</p>
      <p>仅凭 &lambda;<sup>&minus;4</sup> 定律无法覆盖的关键问题：</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>非理想性：</strong> 完整的瑞利–米散射过渡，加上空气的折射率 n(&lambda;)&nbsp;&lt;&nbsp;1，会对朴素截面进行修正。空气在 550 nm 处的瑞利截面约为每个分子 5.1 &times; 10<sup>&minus;31</sup> m<sup>2</sup>。</li>
        <li><strong>多次散射：</strong> 对于海平面、晴空的单次散射是很好的近似，但在大气深厚处（如高海拔、暮光）多次散射和拉曼散射（气辉，CH<sub>4</sub>/OH 谱线）会有贡献。</li>
        <li><strong>光谱源与探测：</strong> 天空的颜色是太阳光谱（&asymp; 5777 K 黑体，经大气吸收修正）、散射相函数、大气透过率与人眼 L/M/S 锥体敏感度共同卷积的结果——这正是它看起来是蓝色而非紫色的原因，尽管紫光散射更强。</li>
        <li><strong>气溶胶与湿度：</strong> 气溶胶（r &sucr; &lambda;）的米氏散射几乎与波长无关，会叠加一层白色成分；这就是为什么有霾的天空看起来更淡。</li>
      </ul>
      <p>简言之：偶极极限下的瑞利散射，与太阳光谱辐射度及明视觉响应卷积，再叠加米氏散射和多次散射修正，便决定真实天空的色调。</p>
    </article>
  </section>

</main>

<p class="note">Single-file presentation · no external resources · 单文件展示页，无任何外部资源</p>

<script>
  function setView(view) {
    var main = document.getElementById('content');
    main.className = (view === 'both') ? 'both' : (view + '-only');
    ['btn-both', 'btn-en', 'btn-zh'].forEach(function (id) {
      document.getElementById(id).classList.remove('active');
    });
    document.getElementById('btn-' + (view === 'both' ? 'both' : view)).classList.add('active');
  }
</script>

</body>
</html>
"}

